  "body": "# Objective\r\n\r\nZed can provision dev containers, and Zed can open projects over SSH, but the two could not be combined. This PR lets you open a project on a remote server over SSH and then reopen it inside a dev container built by *that server's* container engine. This is in references to #56252 and #59500.\r\n\r\n> A remote dev container is a dev container opened from within an already-open SSH remote project, provisioned on that project's host.\r\n\r\n## Solution\r\n\r\n1. `DockerConnectionOptions` gained a `host` field\r\n\r\n```rust\r\npub enum DockerHost {\r\n    Local,\r\n    Ssh(SshConnectionOptions),\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\n`build_command` already existed, and it emits `ssh -o ControlPath=…`, so the second hop reuses Zed's existing multiplexed master with no second authentication. `start_proxy` still spawns exactly one local child, so the RPC framing is untouched. Nothing in `crates/remote_server/` changes and no new proto messages were added. So far `host` is strictly non-recursive, docker-in-docker and WSL-hosted containers aren't running\r\n\r\n2. Engine invocations, config reads, lifecycle commands, `initializeCommand`, the shell environment, and `id -u` run on the host\r\n3. OCI downloads and template rendering stay local — the client has the HTTP client and credentials — and the staged build context is uploaded, with generated paths translated to the host's\r\n4. The server binary and extension directories are streamed in over `docker exec` rather than `docker cp`, which cannot reach a daemon on another machine. Directories travel as an in-process tar stream, so the client needs no `tar` binary\r\n5. Port forwarding composes with the host's `ssh -L`, pointed at the container's address on the daemon's machine (probed once at connect time), since an arbitrary container port is not published\r\n6. Connection identity, display name, telemetry, and persistence all carry the host, so two containers with the same id on different machines no longer collide. `connection_type()` gains `docker-ssh` / `podman-ssh` — dashboards splitting on that string will need updating to keep counting these as docker\r\n\r\n## What's not yet included:\r\n\r\n\r\n1. Declared `forwardPorts` / `appPort` do not auto-forward them to the client\r\n2. Programmatic port forwarding (debugger, REPL) needs host to route to container addresses — true for Linux bridge networking, not for Docker Desktop's VM\r\n3. `updateRemoteUserUID` only applies when the configuration declares `features`, because the id rewrite is a build layer on top of the features image (This is a feature of the existing dev container implementation)\r\n4. Windows clients skip the id update entirely (`cfg(not(windows))`)\r\n- The container must have `tar` for extension sync\r\n- Collab-shared projects are refused: the container would have to be built on another user's machine\r\n\r\n## Testing\r\n\r\nUnit and integration tests were added alongside each change, covering: serde round-tripping without the new field, argv equality (byte identical), argv for the proxy and for interactive commands, pool reuse and rebuild of the host connection, kill semantics leaving a shared SSH master alive, host-path translation for the build context and compose overrides, config and `.env` reads travelling the host connection, id probing on the host, port-forward composition and its three refusal cases, directory upload through a real local shell standing in for the container, persistence round-tripping including password stripping, and the `OpenDevContainer` action reaching the modal for a remote project.\r\n\r\nCurrently tested: macOS client → Linux server\r\n\r\nNot tested: Windows clients, podman as the host engine, Docker Compose against a real remote host (covered by tests only), and hosts whose engine is Docker Desktop rather than a Linux daemon\r\n\r\nReviewers can exercise this by opening any project with a `.devcontainer/` on an SSH remote and accepting the suggestion, or via \"Connect Dev Container\" in the Remote Projects modal.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Self-Review Checklist:\r\n\r\n- [X] I've reviewed my own diff for quality, security, and reliability\r\n> Suggested rule: Never build an engine or host command with `util::command::new_command` / `Command::new`, anything that acts on the machine a dev container is built on must go through `DevContainerHost::command` or a `DockerClient` method\r\n- [X] Unsafe blocks (if any) have justifying comments\r\n- [X] The content adheres to Zed's UI standards ([UX/UI](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#uiux-checklist) and [icon](https://github.com/zed-industries/zed/blob/main/crates/icons/README.md) guidelines)\r\n- [X] Tests cover the new/changed behavior\r\n- [X] Performance impact has been considered and is acceptable\r\n> every engine invocation costs an extra hop, and connect-time probing does several. ControlMaster reuse keeps this to round-trip latency rather than reconnection, and the local path is unchanged\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nRelease Notes:\r\n\r\n- Added support for opening dev containers on a remote host over SSH\r\n",
